  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2017 Figshare dataset offers a survey of how ORCID was being used in book workflows, with the main contribution lying in documenting an emerging publishing practice rather than in measured citation impact. The record shows modest direct use so far: 6 views, 0 downloads, 0 OpenAlex citations, and no scite or event-data mentions.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset was created to explore how ORCID iDs and linked metadata were being adopted beyond journals and into book publishing workflows. Its topical profile centers on workflow and related computer-science and web/database concepts, which fits the practical, infrastructure-oriented nature of the work.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The available impact signals are limited but clear. Figshare reports 6 views and no downloads, while OpenAlex records no citations. scite shows no supporting or contradicting citations, and public-mention tracking does not currently surface any event-data mentions. Taken together, this suggests that the dataset has had only light visible uptake in the indexed record so far.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">That quiet footprint should be read cautiously: the dataset is open access, deposited in Figshare, and discoverable through repository registries and OpenAIRE, which supports access even if downstream reuse is not yet visible in citation or mention data. The record also includes a related identifier linking it to the same dataset DOI and a reference to the ORCID community site, reinforcing its connection to a specific standards and workflow context.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the strongest evidence here is not broad influence but documented availability and domain relevance. This is a survey dataset intended to inform ORCID\u2019s role in book publishing workflows, and the present record shows early-stage reach rather than a substantial, measurable citation or discussion footprint.</p>",
